<p class="heritage-longread__dek">A thirteen-generation story, from a Yorkshire tannery yard in the storms of the seventeenth century &mdash; through the woollen villages of the Aire valley, across the Atlantic to the Saskatchewan dust, and west again into the Alberta oilfields and the Peace Country &mdash; to three children in whom the Haiste line meets four others. Where the records and the inherited genealogy disagree, the records win; see the <a href="#lr-notes">Notes</a> for what is confirmed, what was overturned, and what is still open.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-the-word-they-never-named"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Prologue</span>The word they never named</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">Yorkshire → Saskatchewan → Alberta · c. 1610 to today</p><p>Every long family has a moment when the line nearly ends. For the Haistes, that moment came in the middle of the seventeenth century, when armies marched across Yorkshire during the English Civil War, plague swept the country in 1665, and the worst winters of the Little Ice Age killed two harvests running. In the middle of all of it, a young Yorkshire tanner named Richard kept his household alive long enough to baptize a son in the parish church of Barwick-in-Elmet in the spring of 1641. The son was named George. Three and a half centuries later, that single thread of survival had become an Alberta family of dozens &mdash; a thread that runs from Yorkshire fields through the long transformation of Yorkshire textiles, across the Atlantic in a steamship, into Saskatchewan dust, and west again into the Alberta oilfields and the towns of the Peace Country. And there the Haiste thread does not run on alone. It reaches a woman named Melanie Lyn Haiste, born in Grande Prairie in 1983, and through her it joins four other families &mdash; the Rycrofts of Leeds, and, carried by a man named Thomas Cheesman, the Lakemans out of Holland and the Dochertys and Hebridean McIvers. The Haiste line is not, in the end, a single thread descending to a single heir. The meeting, not the line, is where this story is going.</p><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>They came through.</p></aside><p>The Haistes have no single word for what carried them through. Other families do &mdash; the Dutch say <em>doorkomen</em>, &ldquo;to come through.&rdquo; The Haistes inherited the same idea without ever naming it. You see it in Richard, who survived a civil war. In a Yorkshire clothier named Joseph who married Hannah Padget at Calverley Church in 1802 and raised seven children outside Idle. In Ernest, who left England as a young man for a quarter-section of Saskatchewan grass he had never seen. In Sydney, who pulled his family out of a Depression dust storm and pointed them toward Alberta. In Jim, who lived through nine decades of change and buried his own son.</p><p>This is their story, told one generation at a time, across the world they had to come through.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-richard-haiste-c-1610-c-1670-the-tanner-in-the-s"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter One</span>Richard Haiste (c. 1610&ndash;c. 1670) &mdash; the tanner in the storm</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">c. 1610&ndash;c. 1670 · Barwick-in-Elmet, Yorkshire</p><p>Richard Haiste was born around 1610, almost certainly in one of the small Yorkshire parishes clustered around Leeds &mdash; Barwick-in-Elmet, Hunslet, Tadcaster &mdash; where his surname appears in the earliest surviving church records. It was a world that would look more like an old storybook than a modern nation: thatched cottages along muddy lanes, church bells marking the hours, people who rarely travelled more than a few miles from where they were born.</p><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--right"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( get_stylesheet_directory_uri() . '/assets/img/heritage/haiste/haiste-ch01-tanner-and-son.jpg' ); ?>" alt="A Yorkshire tanner and his young son in the fields" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A tanner and his boy in the Yorkshire fields &mdash; the trade, and the world, Richard was born into. <em>(Illustrative.)</em></figcaption></figure><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>He handed off a living family to the next generation.</p></aside><p>By trade, Richard was a yeoman and a tanner. Tanning was one of the indispensable trades of the seventeenth century &mdash; hides turned, through weeks of soaking in oak-bark pits and patient scraping, into the leather everyone depended on: boots, saddles, harness, soldiers&rsquo; equipment, the bindings of a book. It was filthy, skilled, respected work. Without tanners, an early modern village could not function.</p><p>He lived through the most violent stretch of English history since the Wars of the Roses. Charles I lost his head to Parliament in 1649; for eleven years England had no king at all, governed by Cromwell; the monarchy was restored under Charles II in 1660. Yorkshire was a battlefield through the worst of the Civil War &mdash; armies taking horses and food, burning fields, ransacking churches. On top of war came the Great Plague of 1665, and on top of plague the Little Ice Age, which made his winters colder and his harvests thinner than any in living memory.</p><p>We do not know much about his home life. Family reconstruction suggests his wife was probably Elizabeth Haigh &mdash; a common Yorkshire surname concentrated in exactly the parishes where the Haistes lived. One son is documented for certain: George, baptized in 1641. There were almost certainly others &mdash; the naming traditions of later generations point to a William, a John, perhaps a Margaret &mdash; but the pre-1653 parish registers were spotty, and many were lost when armies ransacked the churches or when the clergy fled and no one was left to write them.</p><p>What we do know is that his family survived. He kept a household running through plague years and war years, and raised at least one son into adulthood &mdash; and that son raised a son, and so on. When Richard died, around 1670, the worst of the century was behind him. He had done the most important thing a seventeenth-century man could do: handed off a living family to the next generation. His name will never appear in any history book. But every Haiste alive today owes their existence to the fact that this one man, in a Yorkshire tannery yard four centuries ago, did not give up.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-george-haiste-sr-1641-1709-recovery"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Two</span>George Haiste Sr. (1641&ndash;1709) &mdash; recovery</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">1641&ndash;1709 · the Yorkshire parishes around Barwick</p><p>George Haiste Sr. was born in the spring of 1641, in a country on the brink of disaster &mdash; the Civil War broke out the next year, and he grew up in the middle of it. By the time he reached adulthood the worst was over: Charles II restored, the Plague of 1665 come and gone, the Great Fire of 1666 cleared away. England was beginning to stabilize, and George&rsquo;s working life was a quiet reflection of that.</p><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--left"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( get_stylesheet_directory_uri() . '/assets/img/heritage/haiste/haiste-ch02-yeoman-patriarch.jpg' ); ?>" alt="Portrait of an older Yorkshire yeoman farmer" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A Yorkshire yeoman of the kind George Haiste Sr. was &mdash; tenant farmer and village butcher. <em>(Illustrative.)</em></figcaption></figure><p>Like his father he stayed in the Yorkshire parishes around Barwick-in-Elmet, working as a tenant farmer and a butcher &mdash; two trades that fed each other and fed the village around them. Tenant farming meant he held the right to work land he did not own; butchering put him at the centre of the community&rsquo;s food economy, slaughtering, curing, and selling at the weekly markets. His was the first Haiste generation to live with the slow conveniences of post-Restoration England, with Leeds &mdash; a short journey off &mdash; already outgrowing its medieval walls into a place where a country butcher could sell to merchants who shipped his goods onward.</p><p>He married a woman named Mary, her surname lost, and together they raised what was, even by seventeenth-century standards, a remarkable family &mdash; parish reconstructions put their children at ten or more: <strong>William, George</strong> (a younger George, in his father&rsquo;s name), <strong>John, Elizabeth, Richard, Philip, Mary, Joseph, Benjamin,</strong> and another George who died young. Large families were the household&rsquo;s economic infrastructure: older children minded the younger, everyone learned the trade, and a wide kin network buffered illness and hard winters.</p><p>George died in 1709, at sixty-eight &mdash; a long life for the period. He took the family Richard had handed him and steadied it, giving the Haiste name to a generation big enough and stable enough that it would never again be at risk of dying out in Yorkshire.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-george-haiste-c-1667-1743-the-quiet-revolution-o"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Three</span>George Haiste (c. 1667&ndash;1743) &mdash; the quiet revolution of precision</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">c. 1667&ndash;1743 · Hunslet &amp; Barwick, Yorkshire</p><p>George Haiste &mdash; son of George Sr., born around 1667 &mdash; grew up in a Yorkshire still recognizably that of his ancestors: small farms, muddy lanes, wood smoke at dawn. He carried on the family trades, butchering and yeoman farming, in the same Hunslet&ndash;Barwick parishes as his father and grandfather. The records of his wife and children are thinner than for George Sr.&rsquo;s, but the reconstructions place him solidly in that orbit.</p><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>He lived in the breath right before it.</p></aside><p>He married, raised a family, and died in 1743, at the dawn of the Hanoverian century &mdash; an ordinary life lived in the last quiet decades before the mills and railways that would, a century on, pull his great-grandsons out of the countryside for good. He lived in the breath right before it.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-georg-haist-c-1715-the-first-pull-of-the-city"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Four</span>Georg Haist (c. 1715&ndash;?) &mdash; the first pull of the city</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">c. 1715 · the family begins to move toward Leeds</p><p>Around 1715 another George was born into the line. The records spell his name variously &mdash; <em>Georg Haist</em>, <em>George Haiste</em> &mdash; because the family name itself was still in slow flux; eighteenth-century spelling was not yet standardized, and the same man could be entered three different ways in three different parish books.</p><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--right"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( get_stylesheet_directory_uri() . '/assets/img/heritage/haiste/haiste-ch04-yorkshire-village-lane.jpg' ); ?>" alt="A lane through a Yorkshire village of stone and thatched cottages" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A village lane in the Aire valley &mdash; the rural Yorkshire the family was beginning to leave. <em>(Illustrative.)</em></figcaption></figure><p>This George was the first Haiste who really felt the pull of the city. Records of his working life describe him as a labourer and sometimes a butcher &mdash; flexible work that could be done in a village or a town. But the larger fact about his generation is that the family began to <em>move</em>. Earlier Haistes had stayed in the parishes around Barwick-in-Elmet; by the time his children were grown, the family was rooted across a wider Yorkshire &mdash; into Leeds, into Hunslet on the city&rsquo;s south side, and into the woollen-textile villages of the Aire valley to the northwest, where the next chapter actually surfaces in primary records.</p><p>What changed was Yorkshire itself. In his father&rsquo;s day Leeds had been an unremarkable market town; by his middle age it was becoming a textile capital, water-driven mills appearing along the Aire and the Wharfe, the woollen villages of Calverley, Idle, Yeadon, and Bingley producing kersey and worsted for the great Piece Halls in Leeds and Halifax. For a man like George, that growth meant choices his ancestors had never had &mdash; and by the next generation, members of his family were established in those Aire-valley woollen villages, one of them a clothier named Joseph who would be the first Haiste whose existence rests not on a compilation but on the register of a parish church.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-joseph-haste-clothier-of-calverley-m-1802-the-fi"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Five</span>Joseph Haste, clothier of Calverley (m. 1802) &mdash; the first Haiste in the primary record</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">m. 1802 · Calverley → Idle, the Aire valley</p><p>The first man in this family whose existence is anchored by a primary register entry &mdash; not a compilation, but the ink-and-paper marriage book of a parish church &mdash; is a Joseph Haste who married Hannah Padget at the Church of St Wilfrid in Calverley on the fourth of January 1802. The register lists him as a <em>clothier</em> of Calverley, his bride as Hannah Padget of the same parish, the witnesses as Thomas Haste and Martha Padget. It has been preserved by the West Yorkshire Archive Service and transcribed on calverley.info. The entry reads, verbatim:</p><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--left"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( get_stylesheet_directory_uri() . '/assets/img/heritage/haiste/haiste-ch05-calverley-parish-village.jpg' ); ?>" alt="A Yorkshire village gathered around its parish church at dusk" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A Yorkshire parish church &mdash; where, in 1802, Joseph Haste&rsquo;s marriage first put the family into the written record. <em>(Illustrative.)</em></figcaption></figure><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>The bedrock entry for this stretch of the family.</p></aside><aside class="heritage-line__quote"><p>1802-01-04 &mdash; HASTE, Joseph, Calverley, Clothier &mdash; PADGET, Hannah, Calverley &mdash; witnesses Thomas Haste, Martha Padget.</p></aside><p>This is the bedrock entry for this stretch of the family. Calverley is a village in the Aire valley northwest of Leeds, in the heart of Yorkshire&rsquo;s <em>woollen</em> district &mdash; distinct from the flax mills of Hunslet south of Leeds, where the inherited genealogy had wrongly placed a man of this generation (see the <a href="#lr-notes">Notes</a>). A clothier in 1802 produced woollen cloth &mdash; weaving on a cottage handloom, fulling, dyeing, finishing for market. Joseph and Hannah settled at Simpson Green, just outside Idle, in one of the last strongholds of the <em>domestic system</em>: families making cloth at home and selling finished pieces through the Piece Halls in Leeds and Halifax. The mills were coming for that trade &mdash; they had already taken cotton &mdash; and by the time the couple&rsquo;s youngest children were born in the 1820s, the domestic clothier was a vanishing figure. But for the first two decades of the century, this was still the life that supported a family like theirs.</p><p>Their children, drawn from the Idle baptism registers, arrived at the steady cadence of a working household:</p><ul>
<li><strong>Thomas</strong> &mdash; born 30 November 1802, almost certainly named for the witness at his parents&rsquo; marriage (likely Joseph&rsquo;s brother or father).</li>
<li><strong>Nathan</strong> &mdash; born 24 June 1811.</li>
<li><strong>Josiah</strong> &mdash; born 1 September 1813.</li>
<li><strong>Joseph</strong> &mdash; born 14 November 1815, baptised 29 March 1818. On the current evidence the most plausible bridge into the next generation &mdash; the man on whose shoulders the line to Saskatchewan rests.</li>
<li><strong>William</strong> &mdash; born 12 February 1818.</li>
<li><strong>Martha</strong> &mdash; born 3 February 1820.</li>
<li><strong>Mary</strong> &mdash; 1822.</li>
</ul><p>Theirs was the world of Yorkshire textiles on the verge of full industrialization &mdash; the Napoleonic Wars keeping wool in high demand, and, in 1812, the Luddite uprisings, when West Riding wool workers smashed the mechanized cropping frames that were taking their livelihoods. Yorkshire was the heart of that movement, and Calverley sat a few villages over from the worst of the violence. The Haistes were part of the population whose hands made the cloth the British economy ran on, in the last years before the mills took the trade over.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-the-victorian-joseph-b-1815-the-family-between-g"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Six</span>The Victorian Joseph (b. 1815) &mdash; the family between generations, between cottage and mill</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">b. 1815 · Simpson Green · between cottage and mill</p><p>The next generation gathered the most stubborn confusion in the inherited tree. On the verified record he is most plausibly the <strong>Joseph born 14 November 1815 at Simpson Green, baptised 29 March 1818</strong> &mdash; son of Joseph Haste the clothier and Hannah Padget, the fourth-born of seven, carrying his father&rsquo;s name. What is directly verifiable about him is, for now, that baptism entry alone. He should have married in the 1830s or 1840s and appeared in the 1841&ndash;1871 censuses; tracking him into those records &mdash; and confirming the link from him to John Haiste two generations later &mdash; is the single biggest piece of primary work left on the Yorkshire end of this family&rsquo;s history (see the <a href="#lr-notes">Notes</a>).</p><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--right"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( get_stylesheet_directory_uri() . '/assets/img/heritage/haiste/haiste-ch06-victorian-textile-mill.jpg' ); ?>" alt="Workers inside a Victorian Yorkshire textile mill" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Inside a Victorian Yorkshire textile mill &mdash; the world that replaced the cottage clothier&rsquo;s trade. <em>(Illustrative.)</em></figcaption></figure><p>What is safe to say is that he came of age as Yorkshire textiles completed their move from cottage to mill. The skilled domestic clothier&rsquo;s trade his father had practised was vanishing; the future was in the steam-powered worsted and woollen mills, and in the towns the railways were stitching together across the West Riding. A man of his age and trade made the hard transition his father&rsquo;s generation never had to &mdash; and the children he raised were almost certainly raised in a town, in the rhythm of factory wages rather than the seasonal cycles of domestic production. At this generation the family completed its move from the rural Yorkshire of parish churches into the urban Yorkshire that would, within two more generations, send a son named Ernest across the Atlantic. The verified record stops, for now, at the 1818 baptism; the story picks up again on solid ground with John Haiste.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-john-haiste-c-1840-after-1891-the-idea-of-leavin"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Seven</span>John Haiste (c. 1840&ndash;after 1891) &mdash; the idea of leaving</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">c. 1840 · Leeds · Pontefract · Otley</p><p>John Haiste was born around 1840, into a Britain at the peak of its global power, the railway network already stitching Yorkshire together. He worked as a labourer, and &mdash; unlike his earliest ancestors, who had stayed in one or two parishes for generations &mdash; he <em>moved</em>, following work through Leeds, Pontefract (a mining and licorice town), and Otley (a mix of industry and farmland). He belonged to a generation that learned to be mobile: the railways made it possible to chase wages across a county, and the new culture of industrial labour made it normal. The old certainty that a man would live and die within sight of the fields he had played in as a child was already gone.</p><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--left"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( get_stylesheet_directory_uri() . '/assets/img/heritage/haiste/haiste-ch07-victorian-working-family.jpg' ); ?>" alt="A Victorian working-class family gathered in a humble cottage interior" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A Victorian working household &mdash; John and Jessy Haiste raised eight children in one like it. <em>(Illustrative.)</em></figcaption></figure><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>What if we left?</p></aside><p>He married <strong>Jessy Hobson</strong> in late 1866, in the Wharfedale registration district near Otley &mdash; a Yorkshire woman of remarkable steadiness &mdash; and together they raised nine children, among them <strong>William, John Alfred, Aaron, Sarah Jane, Rose, Herbert, Phyllis,</strong> and <strong>Ernest</strong>. Victorian working mothers like Jessy ran households on razor-thin budgets and absorbed every shock the industrial economy threw at a family &mdash; illness, seasonal unemployment, accidents in the mills. The household ran on women like her.</p><p>John&rsquo;s own parentage is one of the open seams in this story. The most plausible candidate father is the Joseph born 1815 at Simpson Green, who would have been twenty-five when John was born &mdash; exactly the right age &mdash; but the link is not yet confirmed against a primary record (see the <a href="#lr-notes">Notes</a>).</p><p>What is not in doubt is what his lifetime contained: the shift that mattered most to the family&rsquo;s future. Emigration became normal. Between 1846 and 1924 roughly fifty-five million Europeans crossed the Atlantic; steamships made the passage in days, the Canadian Pacific Railway (1885) opened the prairies, and posters appeared in every Yorkshire town advertising free homestead land. John never made the crossing, and most of his children stayed in Yorkshire. But the youngest son, Ernest, born in 1881, would. John&rsquo;s quiet contribution to the Haiste story is that he raised a son willing to ask the question that had begun to enter ordinary working-class English households for the first time in centuries &mdash; <em>what if we left?</em> &mdash; and to act on it.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-ernest-haiste-1881-1968-the-atlantic-crossing"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Eight</span>Ernest Haiste (1881&ndash;1968) &mdash; the Atlantic crossing</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">1881&ndash;1968 · Yorkshire → Assiniboia, Saskatchewan</p><p>Ernest Haiste was born on 16 October 1881 in Pontefract, Yorkshire, into a world that was, for the first time, globally connected &mdash; the telegraph carrying news across oceans in minutes, steamships running on schedules, the Empire at its territorial peak. But the Empire was not for him. His choice &mdash; and it was a choice, made deliberately and not by economic accident &mdash; was to leave it.</p><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--right"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( get_stylesheet_directory_uri() . '/assets/img/heritage/haiste/haiste-ch08-rms-baltic-steamship.jpg' ); ?>" alt="The RMS Baltic, a White Star Line ocean liner, at sea" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The RMS Baltic &mdash; a White Star liner of the kind that carried Yorkshire emigrants from Liverpool to Halifax in the early 1900s.</figcaption></figure><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>He did not just cross an ocean. He carried the entire future of the family with him.</p></aside><div class="heritage-longread__gallery"><p class="heritage-longread__gallery-title">The crossing west</p><div class="heritage-longread__gallery-grid"><figure class="heritage-line__figure"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( get_stylesheet_directory_uri() . '/assets/img/heritage/haiste/haiste-homestead-sod-house.jpg' ); ?>" alt="Settlers in front of a prairie sod house" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A prairie sod house &mdash; the kind of first home a homesteader like Ernest built by hand.</figcaption></figure><figure class="heritage-line__figure"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( get_stylesheet_directory_uri() . '/assets/img/heritage/haiste/haiste-homestead-barn-raising-1922.jpg' ); ?>" alt="Neighbours raising a barn frame, 1922" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A barn-raising, 1922 &mdash; neighbours travelled half a day by horse to get a barn up.</figcaption></figure><figure class="heritage-line__figure"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( get_stylesheet_directory_uri() . '/assets/img/heritage/haiste/haiste-cpr-rockies.jpg' ); ?>" alt="Engraving of a Canadian Pacific Railway train passing through the mountains" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Canadian Pacific Railway through the mountains &mdash; the line that carried emigrants from Halifax to the prairie.</figcaption></figure></div></div><p>Around 1900 he boarded a steamship in Liverpool, crossed the Atlantic to Halifax, and passed through Canadian immigration into a country that was actively recruiting British and European homesteaders to populate the prairies west of Winnipeg. He boarded a Canadian Pacific train and rode it several days across nearly four thousand kilometres of forest, lake, and grassland to southern Saskatchewan &mdash; the Assiniboia district, where the Dominion government would grant a quarter-section (160 acres) to anyone who could break the soil, build a house, and stay three years. He stayed. He broke soil with his hands. He found a wife &mdash; whose name does not survive in the materials gathered here, though her presence anchors the next two generations &mdash; and together they raised <strong>Sydney, Herbert, Roy, Irene,</strong> and others: the first Haistes born on Canadian soil. The 1926 census still finds the family on the land in the Assiniboia district.</p><p>Life on a Saskatchewan homestead in those years was brutal and beautiful in roughly equal measure &mdash; endless sky and grass and wind, a small drafty house of sod or rough lumber, winters at forty below, water hauled by hand from a well that froze every November. But the soil was rich, the wheat grew in volumes Yorkshire farmers would not have believed, and the homestead came with something Yorkshire had never offered: the chance to <em>own land</em>. For a family whose recent ancestors had been tenant farmers, clothiers, and labourers &mdash; people who had spent generations selling their labour to someone else &mdash; a quarter-section of their own was a liberation no previous Haiste had known.</p><p>Ernest lived a long life, eighty-seven years, from 1881 to 1968 &mdash; long enough to watch horses give way to tractors and candles to lightbulbs, and to see his grandchildren grow up Canadian. Every Canadian Haiste alive today traces their beginning to this one man. He did not just cross an ocean. He carried the entire future of the family with him.</p><aside class="heritage-line__quote"><p><strong>The convergence begins here.</strong> When Ernest broke his quarter-section of Assiniboia sod around 1907, he was not the first of this larger family to break Saskatchewan ground. A full generation earlier, in the 1880s, the cleared Hebrideans of Thomas Cheesman&rsquo;s line &mdash; the <strong>McIvers</strong> near Saltcoats, the <strong>Campbells</strong> near Wapella &mdash; had already turned the same prairie soil a few hundred kilometres to the east, and had already begun, as the Haistes soon would, to push on west to Alberta when the prairie would not hold them. Two crossings of the same dust, made decades apart by families who would never meet &mdash; until their descendants married a hundred and thirty years later, and the two prairie landfalls turned out to have been, all along, two tributaries of one river.</p></aside><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-sydney-haiste-1906-1980s-the-dust-and-the-oilfie"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Nine</span>Sydney Haiste (1906&ndash;1980s) &mdash; the dust and the oilfields</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">1906&ndash;1980s · Saskatchewan → Alberta</p><p>Sydney Haiste was born on the Saskatchewan prairie in 1906 &mdash; the first generation of the family born outside England in three hundred years of recorded history. His was the working childhood of any prairie farm kid: chores started young, the land did not negotiate, and by his teens he was the kind of person who did not complain, did not quit, and did not wait for someone else to fix his problems.</p><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--left"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( get_stylesheet_directory_uri() . '/assets/img/heritage/haiste/haiste-ch09-dust-bowl.jpg' ); ?>" alt="A dust storm rolling over a prairie settlement in the 1930s" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A dust storm rolls over the prairie in the 1930s &mdash; the Dust Bowl that drove Sydney Haiste west to Alberta.</figcaption></figure><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>Don&rsquo;t look back, build here.</p></aside><p>Then the 1930s arrived, and the prairies fell apart. Wheat prices collapsed, crop failures stacked on each other, and the drought came &mdash; the worst in the prairies&rsquo; recorded history &mdash; followed by the Dust Bowl, the topsoil Ernest had spent thirty years working drying out and blowing away. Storms of fine black dust buried fences, killed livestock, and pushed thousands of homestead families off the land they had broken with their own hands. Sydney was in his late twenties through the worst of it, old enough to remember when the farm had been viable and young enough to watch it die.</p><p>So sometime in the late 1930s or 1940s he made the decision his father had made one generation earlier: he packed up and went looking for a better province. He went west, to Alberta &mdash; which, while Saskatchewan was buried in dust, was booming. Oil had been struck at Turner Valley before the First World War; the 1947 strike at Leduc, just south of Edmonton, would soon make the province the energy capital of the country; railways pushed north into Grande Prairie, and new refineries, pipelines, and oilfield towns appeared every year. He went where the work was, taking whatever trades came &mdash; farming, carpentry, the early oilfields, mill work &mdash; and built a life. He married, and raised a family that included his son <strong>Sidney James &ldquo;Jim&rdquo; Haiste</strong>, born in 1931 in the worst years of the Depression, and Jim&rsquo;s brothers <strong>Cliff, Bob,</strong> and <strong>Grant</strong>. The Haistes had now made two migrations in two generations &mdash; Yorkshire to Saskatchewan, Saskatchewan to Alberta &mdash; each driven by the same search for land or work that could feed the next generation, each asking the same thing: don&rsquo;t look back, build here. Without Sydney&rsquo;s decision to leave Saskatchewan, the entire Alberta branch of the family would not exist.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-sidney-james-jim-haiste-1931-2018-the-alberta-pa"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Ten</span>Sidney James &ldquo;Jim&rdquo; Haiste (1931&ndash;2018) &mdash; the Alberta patriarch</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">1931&ndash;2018 · the Alberta patriarch</p><p>Jim Haiste was born on 23 April 1931 in Alberta, in the deepest pit of the Depression &mdash; a childhood of not enough money and a lot of teaching by example about how to make do with less. By the time he was a teenager the world had transformed: the Second World War had remade the Canadian economy, and Alberta, with the 1947 Leduc oil strike, was stepping into its modern identity at the exact moment Jim stepped into adulthood. His working life unfolded across the postwar boom; the details of his trade are not fully documented, but his was the life of a working Alberta man of his generation &mdash; physical, practical, steady, focused on his family.</p><p>He married twice. His first wife &mdash; Melanie&rsquo;s grandmother &mdash; was <strong>Margaret E. &ldquo;Betty&rdquo; Funnell</strong>, born about 1930 in Manitoba; together they raised their family across Dawson Creek, Edmonton, and Calgary through the postwar decades. After Betty, he married <strong>Catherine Caroline Barclay</strong> (1936&ndash;2016). Across the two marriages he raised six children: <strong>Patrick</strong> (m. Karen), <strong>Cynthia, Patricia, Daniel (&ldquo;Dan&rdquo;)</strong> &mdash; Melanie&rsquo;s father &mdash; <strong>Eric</strong> (m. Cheraty), and <strong>Chris</strong>. Many had children of their own, and by the 1990s the Haiste name was known from Edmonton to Grande Prairie; the roots Ernest had planted in Saskatchewan at the turn of the century had grown into branches that reached across a province. The Haistes are an Anglican family, deep in prairie agriculture since Ernest&rsquo;s homestead. His home, like many Alberta homes of the time, blended biological and step-family relationships into one shared story.</p><p>Jim&rsquo;s life also held the kind of grief no parent expects to carry: he buried his son Chris, and he buried grandchildren. He carried those losses the way Alberta men of his generation tended to &mdash; quietly, without complaint, with the same posture handed down through generations of Haiste survival, from a Yorkshire clothier he had never heard of and a Yorkshire tanner who lived through plague and civil war four centuries before he was born. He died on 18 August 2018 in Edmonton, aged eighty-seven, having lived his last twenty-three years with Parkinson&rsquo;s disease; his funeral was held at St. Patrick&rsquo;s Anglican Church. He left children, grandchildren, and great-grandchildren whose lives all trace back, in an unbroken chain, to a tannery yard four centuries ago and a Calverley parish church two centuries ago.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-where-the-haiste-river-joins-the-others"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">The Living Line</span>Where the Haiste river joins the others</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">Grande Prairie · the confluence of five families</p><p>Every long family story has a moment when it stops being history and becomes the present. For the Haistes, that moment is now.</p><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>Five families, from four corners of the world, meeting at last in one Peace Country home.</p></aside><p>Among Jim&rsquo;s descendants is his granddaughter <strong>Melanie Lyn Haiste</strong>, born in Grande Prairie in 1983 &mdash; the daughter of Jim&rsquo;s son <strong>Dan Steven Haiste</strong>, born in 1959, and the living point at which the whole Haiste line arrives in the present. Through Dan, and Jim, and Sydney, and Ernest, and the Calverley clothier, and the Yorkshire tanner before them all, she carries generations of Haiste survival: descendant of a tanner who lived through civil war and plague; of a clothier who married Hannah Padget two centuries before her and raised seven children at Simpson Green; of a man who crossed an ocean alone to break virgin Saskatchewan soil; of a man who pulled his family out of a dust storm and pointed them toward Alberta.</p><p>But Melanie does not carry the Haiste line alone, and this is where the shape of the whole family becomes clear. On her mother&rsquo;s side she carries the <strong>Rycrofts</strong> of Leeds and the <strong>Steinkes</strong>, through her mother Lana Lyn Rycroft. And in Calgary in 2016 &mdash; they married on the second of July &mdash; her life joined that of <strong>Thomas Cheesman</strong>, born in Calgary, who carries currents of his own: the <strong>Lakeman</strong> line out of a Dutch polder through his father, and the <strong>Docherty</strong> and Hebridean <strong>McIver</strong> lines out of Ireland and the Scottish islands through his mother. Thomas carries the surname Cheesman not by blood but by choice, from the step-father whose family gave it to him &mdash; the chosen channel into which all of these blood-lines pour. Melanie and Thomas are the co-parents of three children, all born in Grande Prairie: <strong>Patience Kristie-Anne Cheesman</strong> (2013), <strong>Daniel Eric Thomas Cheesman</strong> (2015), and <strong>Faith Lana Lyn Cheesman</strong> (2017). The Haiste line does not narrow to one of them. It passes to all three, together, braided with four other lines &mdash; the Lakeman, Docherty, and McIver through their father; the Haiste and Rycroft through their mother; and the Cheesman name that gathers them all. Five families, from four corners of the world, meeting at last in one Peace Country home.</p><p>None of those ancestors knew Patience, Daniel, or Faith. Richard Haiste could not have imagined an ocean; the Calverley clothier could not have imagined an aircraft; Ernest could not have imagined that his great-great-great-grandchildren would be born in a country whose grocery stores carried fruit from every continent, in towns connected to the whole earth by glass screens that fit in a hand. What every one of them did know &mdash; what every one of them practiced, generation by generation, without ever needing a single word for it &mdash; is the thing that brought their family through four centuries of plague, war, industry, migration, dust, and change.</p><p>They came through.</p><p>Patience, Daniel, and Faith inherit it together &mdash; not only the Haiste share of it, but the whole confluence. The world they will live through is not yet written. But the family has been here before. The evidence &mdash; some verified, some still being rebuilt, all of it real &mdash; says so.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-lineage-at-a-glance">Lineage at a glance</h2><div class="heritage-longread__table-wrap"><table class="heritage-longread__table">
